db/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184972 #edb/ref 1061 30990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184972 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "749703b1-407a-5adb-8fda-1176ec5284b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184972 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185322 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that the singular values of a real matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix $A^T \\! A.$\n\nAlso, since $A^T \\! A$ is a positive semi-definite matrix, the eigenvalues of $A^T \\! A$ must be always non-negative real numbers.\n\nLet's compute the singular values of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$A= [\\begin{aligned}\\,0 & \\,2 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$$\n\nNow, since\n\n- $A$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix, and\n- $A^T$ is a $2 \\times 3$ matrix,\n\nthen $A^T \\! A$ must be a $2 \\times 2$ matrix:\n\n$$\\underset{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2} \\times {\\large 3}}{A^T} \\times \\underset{{\\large 3} \\times \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2}}{A} = \\underset{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2} \\times \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2}}{A^T \\! A}$$\n\nLet's now compute our singular values. First, we calculate $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}\\,0 & \\,2 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}\\,0 & \\,2 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\,0 & \\,2 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we compute the eigenvalues of $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}8-\\lambda & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (8-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -9)(\\lambda -4) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda & =9,\\,4\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{4}=2\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When listing the singular values of a matrix, we usually write them in descending order.\n- If $A$ is a $m\\times n$ matrix, then $A^T A$ is a $n\\times n$ positive semi-definite matrix. This means that $A$ has *at most* $n$ distinct singular values." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185322 #edb/ref 1065 4891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185322 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6230f35e-b526-5e84-be30-86c8cdcbd301" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185322 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415543 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186415579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415543 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186415580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415543 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186415581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415543 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186415582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415543 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186415583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415543 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415543 #edb/ref 1045 "Computing the Singular Values of a Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415543 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186415583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415543 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415543 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415543 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "53b5db0f-521e-5c61-9c0c-fdff9e2691c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415544 #edb/ref 1080 "$8$ and $5$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415544 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415544 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "03cdbbac-81f1-5a5a-8c0b-cc9eada2874c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415545 #edb/ref 1080 "\\sqrt{3},\\,2,\\,0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415545 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1